# ============================================================================
# HOLD/OS — Cloudflare Pages response headers (DEP-1)
#
# Vite copies public/ verbatim into dist/, so this file ships in every build;
# Cloudflare Pages applies it to static responses on both hosting paths
# (Git integration and direct upload). Syntax notes: comments are `#` lines,
# blocks are a path followed by indented `Header: value` lines, and a request
# that matches multiple blocks inherits ALL of them — duplicate header names
# are COMMA-JOINED, not overridden. That joining rule shaped this file:
# every header name below appears in exactly one block per matched path.
#
# DECISIONS RECORDED HERE (the "why" behind every line and every absence):
#
# 1. The CSP is NOT mirrored as a header. The enforced policy ships as the
#    meta tag in index.html (CA-1, the single source of truth — the strict
#    `script-src 'self'` etc. that privacy.spec.ts pins and canary-tests).
#    Multiple CSP sources never merge: a resource must satisfy ALL of them,
#    so a full header copy would add zero restriction while creating a
#    second authority over the same directives that can silently drift.
#    The header below carries exactly ONE CSP directive — `frame-ancestors` —
#    because a meta-delivered frame-ancestors is IGNORED by browsers
#    (CSP3 §6.3). The meta governs everything it can deliver; the header
#    adds only what the meta cannot.
#
# 2. Permissions-Policy has no meta form at all (response header only, per
#    MDN) — this is the exact CA-1 handoff line. The app calls none of these
#    APIs; the header makes that a browser-enforced fact instead of a claim.
#
# 3. X-Frame-Options: DENY is the legacy belt to frame-ancestors 'none'
#    (old browsers ignore CSP framing directives). Same CA-1 handoff.
#
# 4. X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff has no meta form; Referrer-Policy
#    AGREES with the existing `<meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">`
#    (identical value from both channels — belt and braces, never conflict).
#
# 5. Strict-Transport-Security is deliberately ABSENT: CA-1 logged it as
#    optional "once the custom domain is known", and HSTS is a hostname-wide
#    commitment. *.pages.dev is HTTPS-only and already HSTS-preloaded, so
#    the line only becomes interesting post-custom-domain — see
#    docs/DEPLOY.md for when/how to add it.
#
# 6. Cache-Control lives only in the two blocks below. Everything under
#    assets/ is content-hashed by Vite (main-<hash>.js, app chunks, fonts):
#    a content change mints a new URL, so those responses are immutable by
#    construction and cache for a year. index.html is the only entry that
#    must revalidate — it is the file that names the current hashes. No
#    other root-level files exist today (the favicon is a data: URL); if one
#    is ever added, give it its own no-cache rule rather than relying on
#    the platform default.
# ============================================================================

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
